<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black power movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1960s–1980s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Black liberation movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black power movement emerged in the 1960s from the civil rights movement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, reacting against its moderate and incremental tendencies and demanding more immediate action to counter white supremacy. Influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malcolm-x">Malcolm X</a>, whose assassination in 1965 helped ignite it, and by pan-Africanism, Black nationalism and socialism, it saw the Black Panther Party, founded in 1966, as its cornerstone. Its followers founded Black-owned stores, clinics and schools; some groups ran social programs while the Black Liberation Army carried out bombings and murdered police officers. It declined by the mid-1970s and 1980s, but influenced later movements such as Black Lives Matter.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-power-movement.jpg" alt="Black power movement" width="330" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CIR Online · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Panther_demonstration.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The slogan Black power was first used popularly by Stokely Carmichael and Willie Ricks of the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee; on 16 June 1966, during the March Against Fear in Greenwood, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, Carmichael led a chant for it that was televised nationally. After the Watts riots of 1965, SNCC cut its ties with the mainstream civil rights movement, arguing that Black Americans had to build power of their own rather than seek accommodation, and moved from nonviolence towards militancy. In late October 1966 Huey P. Newton and Bobby Seale founded the Black Panther Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Panthers first used open-carry gun laws to protect members and Black neighbourhoods from the police and followed police cars to record brutality. By 1969 they and their allies were the main targets of the FBI's COINTELPRO, singled out in 233 of its 295 authorised Black nationalist actions. At the 1968 Summer Olympics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, Tommie Smith and John Carlos each raised a black-gloved hand during the American anthem.</p><p>In 1970 former Panthers formed the Black Liberation Army to pursue violent revolution. It is believed to have bombed St. Brendan's Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> during a police officer's funeral that year, and its members killed police officers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, San Francisco and Atlanta in 1971 and 1972. A split between Newton and Eldridge Cleaver in 1971 led to four killings, and by the late 1970s the party had fallen apart. In 1985 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> police bombed the house of the group MOVE, killing several adults and children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The movement put weight on education and its own media. The fifth point of the Black Panther Party's Ten-Point Program demanded education that taught Black Americans their true history, and their newspaper, the Black Panther, was among their most effective tools. WAFR in Durham, North Carolina, launched in 1971 as the first public, community-based Black radio station. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> the movement influenced Aboriginal activists from the late 1960s, and the Aboriginal Legal Service in Redfern grew out of that activism. Some have compared Black Lives Matter to it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Black power movement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the diaspora, Pan-African ideas fed the civil rights and <b>Black Power movements</b> in the United States, where Malcolm X founded the Organization of Afro-American Unity in 1964 on the model of the OAU, and the independence and cultural movements of the Caribbean, among them Rastafari, which arose…</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Kuti's politics owed much to his mother, Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti, an anti-colonial activist who led the Abeokuta Market Women's Movement in the 1940s, and to a visit to the United States in 1969, where he met the <b>Black power movement</b> and Pan-Africanist writing.</blockquote><a href="/songs/zombie">Zombie</a></li></ul></section>
